                <pb facs="0701" n="676" xml:id="pg676"/>

                <head>286. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On May 7 Secretary of State Herter briefed the National Security Council on the
                    forthcoming Foreign Ministers meeting. After tracing recent developments in the
                    Western position since the NATO Ministerial Meeting, April 2–4, he outlined the
                    phased plan for German reunification and indicated that there seemed to be
                    evidence that the Soviet Union might make concessions at Geneva to ensure a
                    Summit meeting. A memorandum of the discussion at the 405th meeting of the
                    Council is in Eisenhower Library, Whitman File, NSC Records.</p>
                <p>Later in the day the Secretary addressed the nation to explain the problems that
                    resulted from the continued division of Germany on the eve of the Foreign
                    Ministers meeting. After reviewing the course of events since the Soviet note of
                    November 27, 1958 (see <ref target="#d72">Document 72</ref>), Herter outlined
                    the Western position on Berlin and then gave his views on the Foreign Ministers
                    Meeting and the prospects for a Summit conference. For text of his address, see
                    Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, May 25, 1959, pages
                    735–739.</p>
            </div>
